 ## Architecture
 
-Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
+Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed. The certificates node uses only this interface and never sees the index.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, index preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. Lookup is served from a local embedded SQLite index built on the kiosk from each imported snapshot. The index is private to the registry; no other component opens it.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Build the SQLite index for the new snapshot in a separate staging file, so the active index is never modified in place and partially imported records are never visible to lookups. Check the finished index against the snapshot (record count and snapshot date) before activation. Activate it only after all validation and the index build succeed. An import failure at any step, including index construction, leaves the previous snapshot and its index active and reports a useful error.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters, then query the active index by serial number. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The snapshot date comes from the same snapshot as the index that answered the query.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index built per kiosk from each snapshot | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Lookups must complete from files on the kiosk with no runtime network access (HLD tenets and non-goals), which excludes both network-dependent options. SQLite gives predictable lookups with no service to run; the cost is that each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index when a snapshot arrives, and that cost stays inside this node. |
+| Index activation | Build in a staging file, activate after validation | Modify the active index in place | Keeps the last valid snapshot available if validation or the build fails, and prevents lookups from seeing a half-built snapshot. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+1. Where staging and active index files live on the kiosk, and how an import interrupted by power loss is recovered at startup.
+2. Whether a previous snapshot's index is retained after a successful activation.
+3. Behavior when the active index fails an integrity check at startup or at lookup time.
